@c capability.texi --- the capability table.  Included from cmacs.texi.
@c Says what doc_org/cmacs/cmacs-capability.org says; keep them in step.

@node Capability Table
@chapter The Capability Table

A capability is written once, as a @code{cmacs-brigade-deftool} form,
and every surface cmacs has reads it from the same place: in-process
agents, the MCP server, the D-Bus interface
@code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Tools}, @command{emacsctl tool}, the AI
right-click menu, and the generated reference at the end of this
chapter.

@file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-capability.el} holds no second copy of any
tool's metadata.  @code{cmacs-capability-describe} computes the table
from the registry each time it is asked, and that value is what D-Bus,
the CLI and the documentation are derived from.

@lisp
(cmacs-brigade-deftool desktop-launch
    "Start a program on the desktop."
    ((command string "What to run")
     (wait boolean "Wait for it to exit" :optional t))
  :group 'desktop
  (my/launch command wait))
@end lisp

With no other change, that tool is the MCP tool @code{desktop_launch},
a tool an agent can be granted by name or through the @code{desktop}
group, callable as @code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Tools.Call "desktop_launch"
'@{"command":"foot"@}'} and as @command{emacsctl tool desktop launch
--command foot --wait}, listed by @command{emacsctl tool --help}, and a
section of the reference.

@menu
* Capability Keys::             What a tool can say about itself.
* Capability Structured Parameters::  Arrays and objects.
* Capability Surfaces::         Where a tool is published, and the
                                  rule for destructive tools.
* Capability CLI Rule::         How a tool becomes GROUP VERB.
* Capability D-Bus::            org.cmacs.Editor1.Tools.
* Capability D-Bus Legacy::     The per-subsystem interfaces, as rows.
* Capability emacsctl::         emacsctl tool.
* Capability Documentation::    The generated, drift-guarded reference.
* Capability Migration::        Moving a hand-written family over.
* Capability Reference::        Every shipped tool.
@end menu

@node Capability Keys
@section Keys

Every key is optional.  The first seven predate the capability table
and mean what they always did; a form written before the rest existed
is unchanged.

@table @code
@item :group
Symbol.  Allowlist vocabulary, and the first CLI word.
@item :destructive
Non-nil keeps the tool from read-only agents, and off D-Bus and the CLI
unless @code{:surfaces} names them.
@item :confirm
@code{nil}, @code{ask} or @code{always}: the call goes through the
approval policy first.
@item :async
The body has @code{done} in scope and calls it with the result later.
@item :timeout
Seconds an async call may take (default
@code{cmacs-brigade-tool-default-timeout}, 120).
@item :menu
@itemx :menu-label
Publish to the AI right-click menu, and the text to show there.
@item :surfaces
List drawn from @code{mcp dbus cli menu agents}.  Omitted means all of
them, with the one exception in @ref{Capability Surfaces}.
@item :cli
@code{"GROUP VERB"}, overriding the derived CLI words.
@item :returns
@code{string} (the default) or @code{json}: how a client should render
the result.
@item :example
One sample invocation, shown in the reference and in @option{--help}.
@item :aliases
Extra wire names that answer to this tool, for a capability that used
to be published under another name.
@end table

Parameter types are @code{string}, @code{integer}, @code{number},
@code{boolean}, @code{array} and @code{object}.  The last two are
described in @ref{Capability Structured Parameters}.

@code{(cmacs-capability-describe &optional SURFACE)} returns the table
as a list of plists with @code{:name :description :group :params
:destructive :confirm :async :timeout :surfaces :cli :returns :example
:aliases}, each parameter a plist of @code{:name :type :description
:required} and, where they apply, @code{:default :enum :items
:properties}.  @code{(cmacs-capability-json &optional SURFACE)} returns
the same as @code{@{"version": 1, "tools": [...]@}}, except that a
structured parameter's @code{:items} / @code{:properties} appear as one
@code{schema} member holding its JSON Schema.  In the JSON form absent
values are @code{null} or @code{[]}, never missing, and @code{version}
changes only when a member is renamed or removed, so adding
@code{schema} did not change it.

@node Capability Structured Parameters
@section Structured Parameters

A parameter may be an array or an object.  @code{:items} says what one
element of an array is; @code{:properties} lists the members of an
object, as parameter specs.  Both are optional: without them the value
is free-form.

@lisp
(cmacs-brigade-deftool team-add
    "Define a team of windows."
    ((name string "Team name")
     (members array "Window ids" :items integer)
     (rule object "Who joins" :optional t
           :properties ((app string "App id")
                        (title string "Title regexp" :optional t)))
     (extra object "Anything else" :optional t)        ; free-form
     (steps array "Steps" :optional t                  ; nested
            :items (object :properties ((key string "Key")))))
  :group 'team
  (my/team-add name members (alist-get 'app rule)))
@end lisp

@table @code
@item (@var{name} array @var{doc})
A JSON array of anything.
@item (@var{name} array @var{doc} :items @var{type})
An array whose elements are @var{type}, one of the six type names.
@item (@var{name} array @var{doc} :items (@var{type} @var{key}@dots{}))
An array of arrays or objects; the keys are @code{:items} /
@code{:properties}.
@item (@var{name} object @var{doc})
A JSON object with any members.
@item (@var{name} object @var{doc} :properties (@var{spec}@dots{}))
An object with those members, each required unless @code{:optional}.
@end table

@subheading What a handler receives

Lisp data, in the shape the registry's own @code{json-parse-string}
calls have always used (@code{:object-type 'alist :array-type 'list}):

@itemize @bullet
@item
an array is a @emph{list} of its elements;
@item
an object is an @emph{alist} with @emph{symbol} keys, in the order the
caller wrote them;
@item
@code{nil} is both the empty array and the empty object, and what an
optional argument that was not given is.
@end itemize

So a body reads an object with @code{alist-get} and walks an array with
@code{dolist}.  Where the tool declared @code{:items} or
@code{:properties} the contents are converted and checked as well: a
declared scalar is a string, an integer, a number or
@code{t}/@code{nil}; a required member that is missing is an error; an
@code{:enum} is enforced.  Members an object's @code{:properties} do
not list are kept.  Free-form contents are passed through as parsed,
which means JSON @code{false} is @code{:false} and JSON @code{null} is
@code{nil} there.

The conversion is done by the tool's handler, not by one surface's
dispatch (@code{cmacs-brigade--structured-handler} wraps it at
registration; @code{cmacs-brigade-structured-value} is the function).
So every way of reaching a tool hands the body the same data, and
accepts the same spellings of it:

@table @asis
@item a list or an alist
A provider's or MCP client's arguments, already parsed by the
dispatcher.
@item a vector or a hash table
@code{json-parse-string} with its default types.
@item JSON text
A model that stringified the argument, @command{emacsctl tool
--@var{name} '@var{json}'}, the menu.
@end table

A value that does not fit is refused with a message naming the argument
and the path inside it --- @samp{argument `rule.keys[2]' must be a
string} --- as a failed call a model can read, and as
@code{BadArguments} on D-Bus before anything runs.

@subheading Handing the argument on

A tool that forwards its argument instead of taking it apart (to a
module's command line, a subprocess, a file) turns it back into JSON
with @code{(cmacs-brigade-structured-json @var{value} @var{param})}.
One thing is lost on the way and cannot be recovered: an @emph{empty}
array or object nested inside free-form data has become @code{nil} by
the time a body sees it, and is written back as @code{null}.  A tool
that needs the distinction declares the member, and its type then
settles it.

@subheading How each surface shows it

@table @asis
@item MCP
Its full JSON Schema in the tool's input schema: @code{items},
@code{properties}, @code{required}, nested as deep as declared.
@item in-process agents
An array with its @code{items} schema (ai-glib's
@code{ai_tool_add_array_parameter}); an object as the bare type with
its members named in the description, because ai-glib's parameter model
has no nested properties.
@item D-Bus
A JSON array or object inside @code{json_args}, validated against the
same description.
@item @command{emacsctl tool}
@xref{Capability emacsctl}.
@item the capability table
@code{type} stays @code{"array"} or @code{"object"}, and one more
member, @code{schema}, carries the JSON Schema.
@item the reference
@samp{array of integer}, @samp{object @{app, title@}}, @samp{array of
object @{key@}}.
@item the menu
Asked for as JSON at the prompt, with its shape in the prompt, and
parsed there (@code{cmacs-brigade-read-param}).
@end table

A @code{:menu} tool's @emph{first} parameter cannot be structured: it
receives the clicked thing as text.  Such a registration is refused, so
the menu never offers a tool it cannot run.

@node Capability Surfaces
@section Surfaces

@table @code
@item agents
In-process agent executors.
@item mcp
cmacs's MCP server: external clients, and CLI agents through the relay
and the scoped sockets.
@item dbus
@code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Tools}.
@item cli
@command{emacsctl tool}.  It travels over D-Bus, so a tool on
@code{cli} but not @code{dbus} is on neither.
@item menu
The right-click menu, for a tool that also sets @code{:menu}.
@end table

A tool that gives no @code{:surfaces} is on all five, @emph{except that
a destructive tool is left off @code{dbus} and @code{cli}}.  To put a
destructive tool there, list them: @code{:surfaces '(mcp dbus cli
agents)}.

The reason is scope.  An in-process agent only ever holds the tools it
was built with.  An MCP caller can be handed a scoped socket whose
allowlist is enforced by the C gate in the process that owns the tools.
A D-Bus caller cannot be scoped at all: every process on the session
bus reaches every method of every interface,
@code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Eval} included, and cmacs has no per-caller
policy there to consult.  So the default does not put a destructive
capability one @command{gdbus call} away; its author decides.

This is a guard against accidents, not a security boundary.  Anything
on the session bus can already evaluate Lisp.

@node Capability CLI Rule
@section The CLI Rule

One rule, applied to every tool that does not set @code{:cli}:

@itemize @bullet
@item
GROUP is the tool's @code{:group}.  A tool with no group, or whose
group is one of the words @command{emacsctl tool} keeps for itself
(@code{list}, @code{describe}, @code{call}, @code{help}), is in
@code{misc}.
@item
VERB is the wire name with a leading @code{GROUP_} removed.
@item
Underscores are written as hyphens in both.
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .25 .15 .6
@headitem Tool @tab @code{:group} @tab CLI
@item @code{desktop_launch} @tab @code{desktop} @tab @command{emacsctl tool desktop launch}
@item @code{agent_spawn} @tab @code{agent} @tab @command{emacsctl tool agent spawn}
@item @code{lql_query} @tab @code{data} @tab @command{emacsctl tool data lql-query}
@item @code{memory_search} @tab @code{memory} @tab @command{emacsctl tool memory search}
@item @code{plain} @tab none @tab @command{emacsctl tool misc plain}
@end multitable

Wire names are unique, so two tools can only land on one verb through
the prefix removal --- @code{desktop_launch} and @code{launch}, both in
@code{desktop}.  Then every tool involved keeps its full name
(@code{desktop desktop-launch} and @code{desktop launch}): both stay
reachable and neither silently wins.  @command{emacsctl tool call NAME}
always works by wire name.

@node Capability D-Bus
@section D-Bus: org.cmacs.Editor1.Tools

Registered at @file{/org/cmacs/Editor} by
@file{cmacs/dbus/cmacs-dbus-iface-tools.c}.

@table @code
@item List () -> s
The table as JSON, filtered to the tools on @code{dbus}.
@item Describe (s name) -> s
One tool's entry as JSON.
@item Call (s name, s json_args) -> s
The tool's result.  @var{json_args} is a JSON object, or empty.
@end table

A failed call is a D-Bus error named
@code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Error.@var{KIND}}:

@table @code
@item NoSuchTool
Nothing is registered under that name.
@item NotOnSurface
The tool is not published on D-Bus.
@item BadArguments
The arguments did not validate; the message names the argument.
@item ToolFailed
A hook vetoed it, confirmation refused it, or the handler failed.
@item Timeout
An async tool did not finish within its @code{:timeout}.
@item ServiceStopped
The D-Bus service stopped while the call was pending.
@end table

@subheading Gate order

The same order as MCP, where MCP has the gate:

@enumerate
@item
The tool exists.
@item
@emph{Surface.}  Its effective @code{:surfaces} include @code{dbus}.
@item
@emph{Scope.}  Nothing to check (@pxref{Capability Surfaces}).  On MCP
this is the allowlist of the scoped socket.
@item
@emph{Arguments.}  The JSON object validates against the typed
parameters.
@item
The shared gates of every tool call:
@code{cmacs-brigade-before-tool-call-functions} may veto, then the
confirmation policy.
@end enumerate

@subheading Arguments

The caller's two strings reach Lisp as two string literals, escaped
with @code{cmacs_dispatch_lisp_escape} and read inside the dispatch's
@code{condition-case}.  The JSON is parsed in Lisp by
@code{json-parse-string}, and its values reach the handler as values.
No caller text is ever part of a Lisp form.

Validation is strict here, unlike the lenient coercion a model gets:

@itemize @bullet
@item
A member that is not a parameter is an error.  The usual cause is a
misspelt flag, and ignoring it would run the tool with a default nobody
asked for.
@item
A required parameter with no default must be present.  JSON
@code{null} counts as absent.
@item
@code{array} accepts a JSON array, @code{object} a JSON object, and
either accepts JSON text of one.  The contents are checked against
@code{:items} / @code{:properties} by the same function a handler's
arguments go through, so D-Bus refuses exactly what the tool would have
refused, only earlier.
@item
@code{integer} accepts an integer, a float with no fraction, or a
string of digits.  @code{number} accepts a number or a numeric string.
@code{boolean} accepts @code{true}/@code{false} (and the strings).
@code{string} accepts a string or a number; an array or object given
for a string parameter becomes its JSON text.
@item
A value outside the parameter's @code{:enum} is an error.
@end itemize

@subheading Confirmation

Not bypassed, and never a prompt.  A dispatch binds
@code{inhibit-interaction}, so there is nothing to prompt with.  A
@code{:confirm} tool runs when @code{cmacs-brigade-auto-approve} covers
it (the default, @code{t}, covers everything) or when a registered
approval handler says yes; otherwise the call is refused with
@code{ToolFailed} and a message naming
@code{cmacs-brigade-auto-approve}.  This is the same path an MCP caller
takes.

(The older @code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Brigade} interface force-approves
its own calls.  That is its behaviour, unchanged; the generic interface
does not copy it.)

@subheading Async tools

@code{Call} is the only method.  For an @code{:async} tool the reply is
simply sent later, when the tool calls @code{done}.

The alternative was @code{CallAsync -> token} plus a
@code{ToolFinished(token, result)} signal.  The held reply was chosen
because:

@itemize @bullet
@item
A caller does not need to know which tools are async.  @command{gdbus
call} and every binding's ordinary method call work for all of them.
@item
A signal is a broadcast: every process on the bus would be handed every
tool's result.  A method reply goes to its caller.
@item
Exactly-once has a single owner.  The invocation sits in a table keyed
by an integer token; answering it means taking it out, and whoever
comes second finds nothing.  A second @code{done}, a @code{done} after
the timeout, and a timeout after @code{done} are all no-ops.
@end itemize

The cost is that the caller's method timeout has to cover the tool.
The table carries each tool's @code{timeout} so a client can set it;
@command{emacsctl} does.  A caller that gives up early loses only its
own answer.

Nothing waits on the editor's side.  @code{Call} starts the tool and
returns to the main loop --- which under @option{--gowl} is the
compositor's --- and the reply is sent from
@code{cmacs-capability--dbus-reply} when the tool or its timeout fires.
(The provider-facing @code{cmacs-brigade-call-tool} still waits in
@code{sit-for}, because a provider's tool contract is synchronous;
@code{cmacs-brigade-call-tool-async} is the non-blocking twin the RPC
surface uses, sharing every gate with it.)

@example
gdbus call --session --dest org.cmacs.Editor \
      --object-path /org/cmacs/Editor \
      --method org.cmacs.Editor1.Tools.Call \
      calc_eval '@{"expression": "2/3*4"@}'
@end example

@node Capability D-Bus Legacy
@section D-Bus: The Legacy Interfaces

Before @code{Tools} there was a method per capability on a
per-subsystem interface:
@code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Compositor.ListClients},
@code{.Gsurf.Navigate}, @code{.BufferManager.List}, 313 of them.
Scripts and @command{emacsctl} call them, so every interface name,
method name and signature is exactly what it was.  What changed is
behind them.  Each method used to carry its own body --- a Lisp form
built with @code{printf} in C --- which was a second copy of what a tool
now does, and had drifted from it: no redaction (a window the rules
exclude was hidden from @code{gowl_list_clients} and listed by
@code{Compositor.ListClients}), no argument validation, a main thread
held for as long as a model or @command{grep} took, and several that
called functions nobody had loaded.

@subheading One table

@file{cmacs/dbus/cmacs-dbus-forward-table.h} has a row for each method
that is served from the capability table:

@example
FWD ("Compositor", "FocusClient", "gowl_focus_client", "pattern,by?", "raw", LEGACY)
FWD ("BufferManager", "List", "@@buffer-list", "", "text", 0)
@end example

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .8
@headitem Field @tab Meaning
@item interface
@tab What follows @code{org.cmacs.Editor1.}
@item method
@tab The D-Bus method.  Its signature stays in the introspection XML of
the @file{cmacs-dbus-iface-*.c} that registers it.
@item target
@tab A tool's wire name, or @code{@@NAME} for the function
@code{cmacs-dbus-method-NAME} in
@file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-dbus-forward.el}.
@item argument map
@tab One item per D-Bus input argument, in order: the tool parameter it
becomes.
@item reply
@tab How the result becomes the method's declared reply.
@item flags
@tab @code{0}, @code{LEGACY}, @code{APPROVE}.
@end multitable

Every interface's method handler begins with a call to
@code{cmacs_dbus_forward}.  For a method with a row,
@file{cmacs-dbus-forward.c} builds one JSON object from the typed
arguments, parks the invocation under an integer token, and evaluates
@code{(cmacs-dbus-forward-call TOKEN TARGET JSON REPLY SIGNATURE
FLAGS)}.  A tool target goes through the gates of @code{Tools.Call} ---
existence, surface, argument validation, the before-call hooks, the
confirmation policy --- and the same
@code{cmacs-brigade-call-tool-async}, so an @code{:async} tool is
answered when it calls @code{done} and nothing waits on the main
thread.  The per-method C is gone: 188 methods are rows, and the
interfaces' files lost about three thousand lines.

Argument map items:

@table @code
@item name
Pass the argument as member @var{name}.
@item name?
Leave it out when it is ``not given'': an empty string, array or dict,
or a number <= 0.  D-Bus has no optional arguments, and these methods
have always spelt ``the default'' that way.
@item name?:VALUE
Send @var{VALUE} instead of leaving it out: the method's own historical
default, where the tool has none.
@item -
Ignore the argument.
@item name=VALUE
A constant; consumes no argument (@code{WindowManager.SplitRight} is
@code{split_window} with @code{direction=horizontal}).
@end table

The types carried are @code{s}, @code{b}, the integers, @code{d},
@code{as} (a JSON array) and @code{a@{ss@}} (a JSON object).  A method
with any other input type cannot be a row.

The tool's text, or the method function's value, is turned into the
method's own out signature, so the wire type is what it always was:

@table @code
@item text
As it is.
@item raw
A printed Lisp string is un-printed.  Many migrated tools answer in
their MCP handler's shape (@code{"focused"} with its quotes); the D-Bus
method always sent the text.
@item prin1
The value printed, for the methods that always sent it printed.
@item lines
The non-empty lines, for a reply of type @code{as}.
@item ok
For a reply of one boolean: true when the call succeeded, @emph{false}
when the tool failed --- a missing buffer, an excluded one, a refused
confirmation.  Bad arguments and a timeout are still errors.
@end table

A reply that is one string is sent verbatim.  Any other type is written
as GVariant text and parsed by the C side against the method's declared
signature, so a value of the wrong shape is a failed call and never a
reply of the wrong type.

@subheading What a forwarded method inherits

@itemize
@item
@strong{Redaction.}  The tool's, which is the point:
@code{Compositor.ListClients}, @code{.FocusedClient} and
@code{.FindClient}, @code{BufferManager.List},
@code{WindowManager.Other}, the @code{Gsurf} methods and
@code{Edit.GetContent} show what the MCP tool of the same capability
shows.  A D-Bus caller is always treated as model-facing
(@pxref{CMacs Redact}).
@item
@strong{Validation.}  Strict, as for @code{Tools.Call}:
@code{Compositor.SetLayout "spiral"} is @code{BadArguments}, where it
used to answer @code{"layout set"}.
@item
@strong{The confirmation policy.}  A @code{:confirm} tool runs when
@code{cmacs-brigade-auto-approve} covers it (the default does).  Turn
that off and @code{Gsurf.EvalJs} is refused over D-Bus exactly as it is
over MCP.
@item
@strong{Errors.}  A failure is the D-Bus error
@code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Error.KIND} with the kinds of @code{Tools.Call}.
The old bodies used the bare name @code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Error}, and
several returned failure as an ordinary result beginning @code{error:}
(@code{Calc.Eval}, @code{Brigade.*}, @code{DbExplorer.*}); those are
errors now.
@item
@strong{Async.}  @code{Ai.Prompt}, @code{Ai.Call}, @code{Ai.Research},
@code{Project.Grep}, @code{DbExplorer.Query} and the other methods
whose tool is @code{:async} no longer hold the main thread.  The
caller's method timeout has to cover the work.
@end itemize

@subheading The legacy-reach grant

A @code{:destructive} tool is not on the @code{dbus} surface unless it
says so (@pxref{Capability Surfaces}).  A legacy method that has always
performed such an action keeps doing it: its row carries
@code{LEGACY}, which lets @emph{that one named method} reach the tool.
Nothing new is opened --- the method existed and did this before --- and
@code{Tools.Call} and @command{emacsctl tool} are not affected:
@code{Tools.Call gowl_set_layout} is still @code{NotOnSurface} while
@code{Compositor.SetLayout} works.  A row may carry the flag only when
its tool really is off @code{dbus};
@code{cmacs-dbus-forward-test-legacy-reach-is-exact} fails otherwise, so
this table is complete and cannot grow by accident:

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .8
@headitem Interface @tab Methods with the grant (and their tool)
@item @code{Compositor}
@tab @code{Spawn} (@code{gowl_spawn}), @code{FocusClient} (@code{gowl_focus_client}), @code{CloseClient} (@code{gowl_close_client}), @code{SetClientGeometry} (@code{gowl_set_client_geometry}), @code{SetLayout} (@code{gowl_set_layout}), @code{WorkspaceSwitch} (@code{gowl_workspace_switch})
@item @code{Monitor}
@tab @code{SetTransform} (@code{gowl_set_monitor_transform})
@item @code{BufferManager}
@tab @code{Kill} (@code{kill_buffer}), @code{Create} (@code{create_buffer}), @code{Save} (@code{save_buffer})
@item @code{WindowManager}
@tab @code{SplitBelow} (@code{split_window}), @code{SplitRight} (@code{split_window}), @code{Delete} (@code{delete_window})
@item @code{Edit}
@tab @code{EditExact} (@code{edit_buffer}), @code{Replace} (@code{replace_in_buffer})
@item @code{Gsurf}
@tab @code{EvalJs} (@code{gsurf_eval_js}), @code{Snapshot} (@code{gsurf_snapshot}), @code{PrintPdf} (@code{gsurf_print_pdf}), @code{PermissionPolicy} (@code{gsurf_permission_policy}), @code{TypeText} (@code{gsurf_type_text}), @code{PressKey} (@code{gsurf_press_key}), @code{SetCookie} (@code{gsurf_set_cookie})
@item @code{Podomation}
@tab @code{Control} (@code{podomation_control}), @code{RunPod} (@code{podomation_control}), @code{EmitEvent} (@code{podomation_emit_event}), @code{LoadFile} (@code{podomation_load_file}), @code{Reload} (@code{podomation_reload}), @code{SetContext} (@code{podomation_set_context})
@item @code{Brigade}
@tab @code{Spawn} (@code{agent_spawn}, approves), @code{Send} (@code{agent_send}, approves), @code{Cancel} (@code{agent_cancel}), @code{Close} (@code{agent_close}), @code{Drop} (@code{agent_drop})
@item @code{DbExplorer}
@tab @code{Execute} (@code{db_execute}), @code{Export} (@code{db_export})
@end multitable

@code{APPROVE} is narrower still.  @code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Brigade} has
always answered the confirmation policy itself, on the reasoning that a
D-Bus caller is the session's owner.  @code{Brigade.Spawn} and
@code{Brigade.Send} keep that; no other interface may carry the flag.

@subheading Methods with no tool

Thirty rows name a Lisp function instead of a tool
(@code{@@buffer-list}, @code{@@clipboard-get},
@code{@@project-read-file}, @dots{}), defined with
@code{cmacs-dbus-forward-defmethod} in @file{cmacs-dbus-forward.el}.
They are the methods whose typed reply a tool's text cannot reproduce
(@code{BufferManager.List} is @code{as}, @code{.Find} is @code{b},
@code{.PointFor} is @code{x}) or that have no tool at all, and that show
something of the screen: buffer names, file names, clipboard text, a
line of a buffer.  Their arguments arrive as values, never spliced into
a form, and each asks the redaction contract.

Some also fix a method that had never worked:
@code{FrameManager.List} formatted a frame's name with @code{%d}, and
@code{Package.List} read @code{package-alist} without loading
@file{package.el}.  (@code{Debug.ProfilerReport} and
@code{Gsurf.Current}, @code{.List}, @code{.ListActions} and
@code{.ListContainers} failed on functions that were not loaded; their
tools load them.)

@subheading What stays hand-written

125 methods keep their body.
@command{admin/cmacs-capability-audit.sh --list dbus} prints each with
its status, and
@code{cmacs-dbus-forward-test-every-method-is-accounted-for} fails for a
method that is neither a row nor on the reviewed list in that test
file.

@multitable @columnfractions .18 .42 .4
@headitem Kind @tab Methods @tab Why
@item Privileged
@tab @code{Editor1.Eval} and the rest of the root interface,
@code{Bacon.*}, @code{Crispy.*}, @code{Eshell.Eval}, @code{Input.*},
@code{Podomation.EvalDsl} and @code{.ReplEval},
@code{Compositor.RunMacro}, @code{.RunKeybind} and @code{.Hotkey},
@code{Cpatch.*}, @code{Cintrospect.*}
@tab They run code or type input the caller supplied.  The same set
stays in C on MCP.
@item Compositor state
@tab The other @code{Compositor} methods, @code{Monitor.Info},
@code{.Modes}, @code{.Set*}
@tab Configuration, layout numbers, modes, outputs --- not a window's
title --- and most go straight to gowl in C under the compositor lock.
@code{RunMacro} also records its trigger as @code{dbus}, which the tool
does not.
@item Reply cannot be reproduced
@tab @code{Edit.SetContent}, @code{Edit.InsertOrg},
@code{SecondBrain.Ingest} and @code{.Migrate}, @code{Lql.*},
@code{Project.WriteFile}, @code{Speech.*}, @code{Audio.Record},
@code{Video.Snapshot}
@tab The tool's arguments or text are not the method's.  @code{Lql.*}
is redacted where every query is, at the snapshot;
@code{Video.Snapshot} asks the capture rule in its form.
@item Acts, answers @code{ok}
@tab @code{Bookmark.Set}, @code{.Jump}, @code{.Delete}; the
@code{Clipboard} writes; @code{Config.Set}, @code{.Theme}, @code{.Font},
@code{.Mode}; @code{File.Open}, @code{.Save}, @code{.Close};
@code{Nav.*}; @code{Package.Install}, @code{.Remove}, @code{.Refresh};
@code{Project.Compile}, @code{.NextError}, @code{.PrevError};
@code{Search.Search}, @code{.Replace}; @code{Text.Insert},
@code{.Append}; @code{ProcessManager.*}; @code{Watch.*};
@code{Instance.*}; @code{Events.Categories}, @code{.EventTypes};
@code{Brigade.Agents}; @code{SecondBrain.Watch}
@tab Nothing of the screen is in the reply, and no tool has the
method's exact behaviour.
@item Not redacted
@tab @code{VC.Status}, @code{.Diff}, @code{.Log}, @code{.Blame};
@code{Events.Recent}
@tab Known gaps (@pxref{CMacs Redact}).
@end multitable

@node Capability emacsctl
@section emacsctl tool

@example
emacsctl tool list [--group GROUP] [--json]
emacsctl tool describe NAME [--json]
emacsctl tool call NAME [--ARG VALUE ...] [--json '@{...@}']
emacsctl tool GROUP VERB [--ARG VALUE ...] [--json '@{...@}']
emacsctl tool --help            # every group and verb, live
emacsctl tool GROUP VERB --help # one tool's flags, live
@end example

@command{emacsctl} has no list of tools.  Every form above asks the
running editor for @code{Tools.List} and builds the verbs, the typed
flags and the help text from the answer.  The instance being talked to
is the source of truth: two editors with different tools give two
different @command{emacsctl tool --help} outputs, and a tool defined in
your init is a subcommand as soon as it is defined.  When no editor
answers, @option{--help} says so and shows the static usage.

Flags are typed by the tool's parameter list:

@table @asis
@item @code{string}
@option{--name VALUE} or @option{--name=VALUE}; sent as a string even
if it is digits.
@item @code{integer}, @code{number}
@option{--name 3}; anything else is a usage error before a call is
made.
@item @code{boolean}
@option{--name} (true), @option{--name false}, @option{--no-name}.
@item @code{object}
@option{--name '@{"key": 1@}'}: a JSON object, and nothing else.
@item @code{array}
One element per use of the flag, or the whole array once as JSON.
@end table

An array flag is repeated, once per element: @option{--members 3
--members 4} is @code{[3, 4]}.  The element is typed by the array's
item type when that is a scalar (so @option{--tags 42} is the string
@code{"42"} for an array of strings, and @option{--members three} is a
usage error for an array of integers); for an array of objects, or one
that does not say, each element is JSON.  A value that is itself a JSON
array is taken as the whole array --- @option{--members '[3, 4]'} ---
and the two forms can be mixed.  The first use of an array flag
replaces whatever @option{--json} gave for it; later uses append.

@example
emacsctl tool team add --name raid --members 3 --members 4 \
         --rule '@{"app": "foot"@}' \
         --steps '@{"key": "a"@}' --steps '@{"key": "b"@}'
@end example

The one ambiguity: an element of an array of strings that is itself the
text of a JSON array (@option{--tags '["a"]'}) is read as the whole
array.  Pass such an element inside @option{--json}.  @option{--help}
shows @samp{--members INT@dots{}}, @samp{--rule JSON} and, for an
object that declares its members, their names.

A hyphen in a flag stands for an underscore in the parameter name
(@option{--max-rows} is @code{max_rows}).  @option{--json '@{...@}'}
supplies the whole argument object; flags given beside it override its
members.  A tool parameter whose name collides with a global option
(@option{--timeout}, @option{--host}, @option{--instance},
@option{--output}, @option{--config}, @option{--context},
@option{--watch}, @option{--quiet}) or is itself called @code{json} has
to be passed inside @option{--json}.

Exit status: 0 on success; 2 when the arguments are refused, here or by
the editor; 3 when the editor has no @code{Tools} interface; 4 when no
instance is found; 1 for any other tool failure.  A tool with
@code{:returns json} is rendered as a document, so @option{-o yaml} and
@option{-o json} work on it.

@subheading The verbs that predate the table

@cindex emacsctl, verb aliases
@command{emacsctl} had about two hundred verbs before it had
@code{tool}: table rows in @file{cmacs/emacsctl/ctl-cmd-*.c}, each
naming one hand-written D-Bus method.  The 110 of them whose capability
is a tool on the @code{cli} surface are now @emph{aliases} of the
generic path.  @samp{emacsctl calc eval '2+2'} is spelt as it always was
and prints what it always printed, and what it does is @samp{Tools.Call
"calc_eval" '@{"expression": "2+2"@}'}.

An alias is one row of @code{alias_specs} in
@file{cmacs/emacsctl/ctl-cmd-alias.c}:

@example
@{ "calc_convert_units", "expression units", NULL, CTL_ALIAS_TEXT, 0,
  @{ "calc units", "Convert to UNITS, or reduce to base SI when omitted",
    CTL_IFACE_CALC, "ConvertUnits", "s:expression s?:units",
    CTL_REPLY_STRING @} @},
@end example

@multitable @columnfractions .22 .78
@headitem Field @tab Meaning
@item tool @tab The wire name to call.
@item parameters @tab The tool parameter each positional argument is, in
order.
@item defaults @tab @samp{name=value} pairs sent for a positional that
was left out, where the old method supplied a value the tool does not.
@item format @tab How to print the tool's text so the output is the old
verb's (below).
@item seconds @tab The tool's own @code{:timeout} when it is
@code{:async}, so the client's method timeout covers it; 0 otherwise.
@item the old row @tab Verb, built-in summary, D-Bus interface and
method, argspec, reply kind --- unchanged from the table it came from.
@end multitable

The old row is not history.  Its argspec types the positionals, which is
why @emph{running an alias costs no round trip beyond the call}: nothing
about the tool has to be asked for.  @code{Tools.List} is fetched only
for @option{--help}.  And its D-Bus method is the fallback.

@multitable @columnfractions .30 .32 .38
@headitem Format @tab The tool returns @tab Printed as
@item @code{CTL_ALIAS_TEXT} @tab text @tab the text
@item @code{CTL_ALIAS_JSON} @tab JSON text @tab a document (@option{-o
json} / @option{yaml} / @option{table} work); the text if it does not
parse
@item @code{CTL_ALIAS_PRINTED} @tab text printed by @code{prin1}, as its
MCP ancestor did @tab the text with the quoting undone
@item @code{CTL_ALIAS_PRINTED_LINES} @tab the same, one item per line
@tab a list, one row per line
@end multitable

@code{CTL_ALIAS_PRINTED} exists because a migrated family kept its MCP
wire format, and the C handlers behind several of them returned
@code{cmacs_dispatch_eval}'s @code{prin1} of a string ---
@samp{"Profiler started"} with the quotes --- while the D-Bus methods of
the same name returned the bare text.

@emph{Fallback.}  A failed @code{Tools.Call} is retried through the old
D-Bus method exactly when the error proves the tool never started:
@code{UnknownInterface} / @code{UnknownMethod} (no
@code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Tools}: an older cmacs, or a build without the
registry), @code{NoSuchTool}, @code{NotOnSurface}, @code{BadArguments}.
The last three are the gates of the gate order (@pxref{Capability
D-Bus}) that come before the tool runs.  @code{ToolFailed},
@code{Timeout} and everything else are reported: retrying could do the
work twice.  @code{ctl_alias_decide} is the whole rule, a pure function
with a unit test.

@emph{Help.}  @samp{emacsctl calc units --help} is the tool's
description and the tool's text for each argument, from the running
editor's table, with @samp{Same as: emacsctl tool calc convert-units
...} under it.  @samp{emacsctl calc --help} uses the first sentence of
each tool's description and marks the verb @samp{[= tool calc
convert-units]}.  With no editor to ask, the built-in summary is shown
and the page says so.

@emph{What keeps the table honest.}
@file{cmacs/emacsctl/tests/test-ctl.c} checks every row without an
editor: a well-formed tool name, positionals that pair one to one with
distinct parameters, the argument mapping for verbs of every group and
type, the fallback decision, where help text comes from, and that every
verb which is @emph{not} an alias has a recorded reason
(@code{kept_verbs}, beside the table).
@file{test/cmacs/cmacs-emacsctl-alias-tests.el} does what C cannot: it
reads the rows out of the C source and holds each against the generated
reference --- the tool exists, is on @code{cli}, has those parameters
with compatible types, and every parameter it requires is one the alias
always sends.

@emph{What did not become an alias}, and why, is the content of
@code{kept_verbs}: no tool does the same thing; the tool is destructive
and so not on the @code{cli} surface, where @code{Tools.Call} would
refuse it; the tool's answer cannot be turned back into what the verb
printed (@code{get buffers} prints bare names and @code{list_buffers} a
five-column listing; @code{lql query} renders at 200 columns and
@code{lql_query} at 160); or the verb composes its request in the client
(@code{ai prompt} reads stdin and @option{-f} files).

Three things differ from the old verbs, all of them the registry's rules
rather than accidents: a tool that fails makes the verb exit 1 with the
message on stderr, where @code{calc}, @code{db} and @code{brigade}
methods printed @samp{error: ...} and exited 0; results pass through the
redaction table; and an optional number that was left out takes the
tool's default instead of the zero the old wire sent.

@node Capability Documentation
@section Documentation from the Table

@kbd{M-x cmacs-capability-doc} renders this session's registry ---
shipped tools and your own --- as an org buffer.

@file{doc_org/cmacs/cmacs-capability-reference.org} and
@file{doc/cmacs/capability-reference.texi} (@pxref{Capability
Reference}) are the same rendering of the tools the shipped
@file{lisp/cmacs} files register.  They are @emph{generated}:

@example
admin/cmacs-capability-audit.sh --write-docs   # regenerate both
admin/cmacs-capability-audit.sh --check-docs   # exit 1 if stale
@end example

The generator is a batch Emacs with an empty @env{HOME} that loads
every file under @file{lisp/cmacs} containing a tool registration
(found by text, not from a list).
@code{cmacs-capability-test-reference-is-current} runs it with
@option{--check} and fails when the checked-in files differ, so a tool
added without regenerating fails the suite.  A build missing a
subsystem cannot load that subsystem's tool file; the generator then
refuses to write (exit 2) and the test skips, rather than producing a
reference with a family silently missing.

@node Capability Migration
@section Migrating a Hand-Written Family

Most of cmacs's RPC surface predates the table and used to exist three
times: a C handler per MCP tool in @file{cmacs/mcp/}, a D-Bus method
per capability in @file{cmacs/dbus/}, a row per verb in
@file{cmacs/emacsctl/}.  The MCP copy is gone --- the handlers the
registry replaced were deleted, see ``What was deleted, and what holds
the wire'' below --- and @file{admin/cmacs-capability-audit.sh}
measures what is left of the other two:

@example
admin/cmacs-capability-audit.sh               # counts per surface
admin/cmacs-capability-audit.sh --families    # MCP C files, cheapest first
admin/cmacs-capability-audit.sh --hand-written
admin/cmacs-capability-audit.sh --remainder   # what stays in C, and why
@end example

As of this writing: 27 MCP tools in C, every one kept there on purpose
(see ``What stays in C'' below), where there were 284 before 257 of
them became registry tools and their handlers were deleted; 316 D-Bus
methods (132 in the registry); 306 emacsctl verbs (117).  For D-Bus and the CLI, ``in the registry'' means a registered
tool of the corresponding name exists; a destructive one still needs
@code{:surfaces} to be callable through @code{Tools.Call}.

The audit has three statuses for an MCP tool: @code{registry} (a C
handler whose tool the registry also provides: a leftover twin, which
must not exist any more), @code{kept} (hand-written on purpose;
@option{--remainder} prints each with its reason) and
@code{hand-written} (in C, not in the registry, and with no reason
recorded).  The first and last counts are zero, so the MCP row's total
equals its @code{kept} column.  A tool added to a C file without a
decision shows up as @code{hand-written}.

@subheading The worked example: the calculator

@file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-calculator-tools.el} is the calculator's five
tools as @code{cmacs-brigade-deftool} forms, under the wire names the C
handlers used (@code{calc_eval}, @code{calc_eval_symbolic},
@code{calc_convert_units}, @code{calc_list}, @code{calc_describe}).
Nothing that called @code{calc_eval} changes.

What was done, which is the recipe:

@enumerate
@item
@emph{Write the tools in Lisp.}  One
@file{cmacs-@var{subsystem}-tools.el}.  Keep the wire names; where a
better name is wanted, keep the old one in @code{:aliases}.  Copy the
descriptions and parameter names from the C schema.  @code{require} the
subsystem inside the body, not at the top: the file is loaded at
startup so the tools are listed.
@item
@emph{Drop the escaping.}  The C handlers escape each argument and
splice it into a Lisp form, and whitelist the ones that become bare
symbols.  A registry handler receives values, so there is nothing to
escape; validate names because a good error is better than interning
arbitrary text.
@item
@emph{Let errors be errors.}  Do not catch and return @code{"error:
..."}; signal.  Each surface then reports failure natively
(@code{isError} on MCP, a D-Bus error, a non-zero exit).  This is the
one behaviour change: the old calculator tools returned failures as
ordinary results.
@item
@emph{Name the file} in @code{cmacs-capability-migrated-families} with
its @code{IS-CMACS-*} flag, so it is loaded with the registry --- before
the first MCP session lists its tools.
@item
@emph{Declare what it does.}  @code{:destructive t} for anything that
writes, deletes, closes, launches, types, clicks, navigates, records,
spends money, or changes the compositor's or the editor's state.  It is
what an MCP client is shown (a tool without it is published
@code{readOnlyHint: true}), what keeps the tool from a read-only agent,
and what keeps it off D-Bus and the CLI unless @code{:surfaces} lists
them.  @code{cmacs-capability-test-action-tools-are-destructive} fails
for a tool whose name contains an action verb and does not say so; the
reviewed exceptions are in that test file, each with its reason.
@item
@emph{Freeze its wire schema.}  Add the tool to
@file{test/cmacs/data/mcp-wire-schema.json}
(@code{cmacs-wire-schema-freeze-registry-tool}, in
@file{test/cmacs/cmacs-wire-schema.el}) and hold the family to it with
@code{cmacs-wire-schema-should-match}, as
@code{cmacs-capability-test-calculator-family-matches-c} does.  For the
calculator the entry was frozen from the C handlers before they were
deleted.
@item
@emph{Delete the C}, if there was any: handler, schema, registration,
and the helpers nothing else uses.  A registry tool is never published
under a name a C tool still serves (the publisher refuses it, whoever
registered it), so a twin left behind is not a fallback, it is the tool
a client gets.
@item
@emph{Regenerate the reference} (@option{--write-docs}).
@end enumerate

@subheading Asynchronous tools in a family

A tool body runs on the main thread, which under @command{emacs --gowl}
is the compositor's.  It must not wait: no @code{sit-for}, no
@code{sleep-for}, no synchronous subprocess or network call.  Anything
that takes time is @code{:async} and answers from a callback.  Three
patterns are in the tree:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@emph{A callback API exists.}  Call it and hand @code{done} the result:
@code{db_query} and its siblings
(@file{cmacs-dbexplorer-tools.el}), @code{secondbrain_search} with
@code{semantic} (the embedding request is asynchronous; ranking is a
scan of an mmap'd array).
@item
@emph{Only a blocking primitive exists.}  Run it in a child of this
same binary with a @emph{constant} form, pass the arguments through the
environment or a private file, and answer from the sentinel:
@code{vidstudio_export}, and @code{ai_list_models}
(@file{cmacs-ai-models-tools.el}), whose only primitive blocks for up
to thirty seconds per provider.
@item
@emph{The work is a queue.}  Start it and return the job's id; the
caller polls: @code{secondbrain_ingest}.
@end itemize

Arm a timer of your own slightly inside the tool's @code{:timeout}, so
the answer that reports a timeout is also the one that cancels the
work.

@subheading The registry without the brigade

The table is not an AI feature.  Three pieces publish it over MCP, and
none of them needs @option{--with-cmacs-ai} or
@option{--with-cmacs-ai-brigade}:

@table @asis
@item @file{cmacs/core/cmacs-tool-registry.c}
Built with @option{--with-cmacs-mcp} @emph{or}
@option{--with-cmacs-ai-brigade}.
@item the publisher in @file{cmacs/mcp/cmacs-mcp-tools-brigade.c}
Built with @option{--with-cmacs-mcp}.
@item @file{cmacs-brigade-registry.el}, @file{cmacs-brigade-tools.el}, @file{cmacs-capability.el} and the family files
Lisp; always there.
@end table

@file{cmacs-tool-registry.c} is the C mirror of the registry ---
@code{CmacsBrigadeTool}, @code{cmacs_brigade_registry_*} and the
@code{cmacs-brigade--mirror-*} DEFUNs.  It lives in @file{cmacs/core}
because it has two users that are otherwise unrelated: the MCP
publisher, and the brigade's allowlist gate.  It needs GLib and
@file{lisp.h} and nothing from either.  Both @code{syms_of_cmacs_mcp}
and @code{syms_of_cmacs_ai_brigade} call
@code{syms_of_cmacs_tool_registry}; the second call does nothing.

The publisher requires @file{cmacs-brigade-registry},
@file{cmacs-brigade-tools} and @file{cmacs-capability} when a session
is set up --- by name and without @code{noerror}, so that a file that
is missing or signals is an error it can remember (see ``When something
does not load'' below).  @file{cmacs-capability} loads every migrated
family whose @code{IS-CMACS-*} flag is set --- which is the whole
table.
@file{cmacs-brigade.el} is loaded too, for its @code{defgroup} and
error symbol, but its eager-load block (the runner, memory, subagents,
schedules) is guarded by @code{cmacs-brigade-available-p} and stays
out.  @code{cmacs-capability-test-lisp-core-needs-no-brigade} runs
exactly this in a child with the brigade's C entry points removed.

@code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Tools} is not gated on the brigade either:
@file{cmacs-dbus-iface-tools.c} is guarded by
@code{HAVE_CMACS_DBUS_TOOLS}, which @file{configure.ac} defines together
with the object in the cmacs-glib block, and nothing in the file needs
the brigade.  The forwarder for the legacy interfaces
(@file{cmacs-dbus-forward.c}) is built with cmacs-glib too.

What stays behind @code{HAVE_CMACS_AI_BRIGADE} in the publisher's file
is the scoped sockets (@code{cmacs-brigade-scope-open} and friends).
They exist to hold a CLI agent to its allowlist, and the allowlist gate
is the brigade's.  A build without the brigade has one MCP socket and
no agents to scope.

@subheading What was deleted, and what holds the wire

The registry is published by every build with an MCP server, so a
migrated tool's C handler had stopped being the implementation in any
configuration: on every session it was replaced by the registry tool of
the same name.  All it still did was answer when the Lisp side had not
loaded.  Those handlers are deleted --- 257 of them, with their
schemas, registrations and the helpers nothing else used.  By C file
(@file{cmacs/mcp/cmacs-mcp-tools-@var{name}.c}):

@table @asis
@item @file{ai}, @file{audio}, @file{buffer}, @file{calculator}, @file{dbexplorer}, @file{debug}, @file{edit}, @file{gnuseye}, @file{gsurf}, @file{imgedit}, @file{libregnum}, @file{project}, @file{secondbrain}, @file{vidstudio}, @file{window}
Every tool (160).  Nothing is left: each is a stub, until
@file{configure.ac} stops listing the object.
@item @file{gowl}
45 @code{gowl_*} tools and the 8 @code{screensaver_*} tools.  Left:
@code{gowl_screenshot}, @code{gowl_hotkey_send}.
@item @file{cad}
17.  Left: @code{cad_snapshot}.
@item @file{lrgterm}
10.  Left: @code{lrgterm_dump_screen}.
@item @file{shell}
8 @code{podomation_*} tools and @code{video_list}.  Left:
@code{bacon_eval}, @code{crispy_eval}, @code{podomation_eval_dsl},
@code{podomation_repl_eval}, @code{video_snapshot}.
@item @file{eval}
4.  Left: @code{eval}.
@item @file{gi}
3.  Left: @code{gi_call}.
@item @file{process}
1.  Left: @code{send_to_process}.
@item @file{input}, @file{cintrospect}
None; all of it is left.
@end table

@emph{The collision rule lost its exception.}  The publisher used to
let a shipped family's tool take the name of its C twin and refuse
everyone else's.  With the twins gone there is nothing to take: a
registry tool is never published under a name one of the remaining C
tools serves, shipped or not, so a tool called @code{eval} cannot
replace the real one whichever file defines it.
@code{cmacs-brigade-registering-shipped-family} survives as bookkeeping
(which wire names came with cmacs) and grants nothing.

@emph{The in-process server gets the registry too.}
@code{cmacs_mcp_get_internal_server} is a process-lifetime
@code{McpServer} that the cmacs-ai tool bridge
(@code{cmacs-ai-tools-register-mcp-bridge}) reads its tools from.  It
is created from @code{main()}, before @file{startup.el} has put
@file{lisp/cmacs} on @code{load-path}, so the table cannot be loaded at
that point --- and for as long as every registry tool had a C twin,
that server quietly held only the twins.  It now takes its C tools at
startup and the registry on the first use after it, and is brought up
to date whenever a tool has been registered or removed since (the
mirror carries a generation counter; publication replaces and removes
what an earlier pass published and never touches a C tool).  Without
this, deleting the handlers would have taken most tools away from chat
sessions without any file failing to load.

@subsubheading The frozen wire schema

What the deleted handlers promised a client --- each tool's name,
description, parameter names, order, types and required set --- used to
be held by a guard per family that parsed the family's C file.  The C
is what those guards read, so it was frozen first:
@file{test/cmacs/data/mcp-wire-schema.json} holds, for each of the 294
names the C published (the 284 tools and ten @code{lrg_editor_*} second
names), exactly what @code{tools/list} returned from a server that had
only its C tools --- @code{description}, @code{inputSchema} and
@code{annotations} as a client received them --- plus the C file, the
position in it and the configure flags the registration was compiled
under.  It was taken from a real session, not parsed out of the source;
where the old text parsers disagreed with it (they did, over one nested
@code{items} schema and three descriptions) the parsers were wrong.

Every family guard now compares the registry with that file, through
@file{test/cmacs/cmacs-wire-schema.el}:

@example
(cmacs-wire-schema-should-match "gowl_list_clients"
                                :description t :enum t :hints t)
;; gowl_list_clients: parameter 2 was `tag' (integer, optional),
;; is now `tag' (string, optional)
@end example

The file is edited on purpose and never regenerated wholesale.
Changing a published tool's parameters means editing its block in the
same commit, where a reviewer sees a wire change as a wire change.  A
new tool is added with @code{cmacs-wire-schema-freeze-registry-tool}
when it should be held to its schema.

What a registry tool publishes is not byte-identical to what the C did,
and never was required to be: the registry sends a parameter's type and
description and the required set, not @code{enum}, @code{minimum} or
@code{maximum} (those are enforced by the handler), and it documents
parameters the C left bare.  The guards say which of these each family
holds.

@subsubheading When something does not load

Nothing stands in for a registry tool any more, so a tool family that
fails to load is a set of tools missing from every surface.  That is
never silent:

@itemize @bullet
@item
A family that fails --- its file is missing, or loading it signals ---
is caught on its own (@code{cmacs-capability--load-family}), recorded
in @code{cmacs-capability-family-failures} with the error, and the
remaining families still load.
@item
It is reported once as an @code{:error} warning naming the family and
the error.
@item
Every MCP session is told: a @samp{NOTICE} paragraph naming each failed
family is appended to the server's @code{instructions}, which a client
reads in the reply to @code{initialize}, and the same text is written
to the server's log once.
@item
A tool called @code{capability_doctor} is always in @code{tools/list}.
It reports which families loaded, which failed and why, and whether the
C mirror the server publishes from agrees with the registry.
@kbd{M-x cmacs-capability-doctor} shows the same report.
@item
@kbd{M-x cmacs-capability-reload-families} loads the failed families
again after the file is fixed.
@end itemize

If the table itself does not load (the registry, the dispatcher or
@file{cmacs-capability.el}), the publisher remembers the error
(@code{cmacs-mcp-registry-load-error} returns it), says so in the
instructions, and registers a C stand-in for @code{capability_doctor}
that answers with it; the session then has the C tools only.  The load
is tried again for each session, so fixing the file and reconnecting is
enough.

@subsubheading What a client is told about a tool

@code{readOnlyHint: true} and @code{destructiveHint: false} for a tool
that is not @code{:destructive}; nothing for one that is.  Nothing is
the right answer by the MCP specification --- @code{readOnlyHint}
defaults to false and @code{destructiveHint} to true --- and it is what
the C handlers sent too (mcp-glib writes only the hints that differ
from the defaults), but it does mean a destructive tool carries no
@code{annotations} member at all.  A client must read an absent hint as
``may modify'', not as ``unknown''.
@code{cmacs-capability-test-c-hints-are-kept} holds every migrated tool
to what its C handler said: read-only then, not destructive now; not
read-only then, destructive now, with a short list of reviewed
exceptions.

@code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Calc} also remains: it is a public name that
scripts may call, and removing it is a compatibility decision rather
than a cleanup.  It is also what @command{emacsctl calc eval} falls back
to against a cmacs that has no @code{calc_eval} tool.  The
@command{emacsctl calc ...} @emph{rows} are gone as rows: the verbs are
aliases of the tools now (@pxref{Capability emacsctl}), so
@command{emacsctl calc eval} and @command{emacsctl tool calc eval} are
one code path with two spellings.

@subheading What stays in C

Twenty-seven MCP tools, each for one of three reasons.
@command{admin/cmacs-capability-audit.sh --remainder} prints the list
from the same table the audit counts with, so it cannot go stale
silently: an entry for a tool that no longer exists, or that the
registry has since taken, is reported.

@table @asis
@item Privileged: runs code or types input a caller supplied
The allowlist names most of them, and a move changes who reviews a
change to them.  @code{eval}, @code{execute_command}, @code{send_keys},
@code{send_to_process}, @code{gi_call}, @code{bacon_eval},
@code{crispy_eval}, @code{podomation_eval_dsl},
@code{podomation_repl_eval}, @code{gowl_hotkey_send},
@code{cmacs.c.compile_snippet}, @code{cmacs.c.call_handle},
@code{cmacs.c.patch_defun}, @code{cmacs.c.unpatch_defun},
@code{cmacs.c.unpatch_all}.
@item C introspection of the running binary: the handler is the feature
@code{cmacs.c.list}, @code{cmacs.c.symbol_info},
@code{cmacs.c.type_info}, @code{cmacs.c.function_source},
@code{cmacs.c.addr_to_source}, @code{cmacs.c.defun_info},
@code{cmacs.c.stack_trace}, @code{cmacs.c.patch_log}.
@item Image content: the result is a PNG, and a registry tool returns text
@code{gowl_screenshot}, @code{cad_snapshot},
@code{lrgterm_dump_screen}, @code{video_snapshot}.
@end table

@code{gowl_reload_config} was a fourth kind, waiting for a DEFUN that
reloads from gowl's search path;
@code{gowl-reload-config-from-search-path} exists now and the tool is a
registry tool (@file{cmacs-gowl-tools.el}).  None of the twenty-seven
is waiting for anything: each is in C because of what it is.

